  "boxes": [
    {
      "type": "anchorbox",
      "title": "Emotional Intelligence and Scale",
      "description": "Hanuman knew exactly how to scale his approach. He approached allies in a tiny, unthreatening form (Sukshma Rupa) to build trust and offer comfort, but assumed a massive, formidable form (Bikata Rupa) when he needed to defend himself. Emotional intelligence requires adapting your approach to match the needs of the situation.",
      "motivating_text": "",
      "citation": ""
    },
    {
      "type": "gotchabox",
      "items": [
        "The Dative Suffix (सियहिं): In Awadhi, the formal name सीता softens to सिय (Siya). The -हिं (-hiṃ) is the objective marker we saw earlier in मोहिं (to me). So सियहिं means \"To Mother Siya\".",
        "Phonetic Shifts: Sanskrit विकट (Vikata) becomes बिकट (Bikata) via the v → b shift. Also, the causative verb जरावा (Jaraavaa) comes from the root ज्वल् (to burn), meaning \"caused to burn\"."
      ]
    }
  ]
